// A farm that promises more than it produces. At 20 milli eaten per resident
// and 400 milli produced per tick it sustains 20 of them: declaring 24 would
// mean taking on one house more than it feeds, and that house would never eat
// again — the first provider wins the contest, so not even a second farm could
// take it over.
//
// The second farm in the table is identical but with a stock smaller than one
// tick's output: the bottleneck there is not how much it produces, but how
// much it can hold.
//
// The house and the well come **after** the two farms, so the indices in the
// expected report do not move. They are here because since phase 13 the
// cross-table checks also want a table to contain a house at all, as many rungs
// as the house declares, and a provider for every service the rungs ask for: a
// buildings table of nothing but farms would produce four more errors and bury
// the two this fixture exists for.
(
    buildings: [
        (
            id: "farm",
            size: (2, 2),
            cost: 40,
            levels: 1,
            service: Some((kind: "food", range_per_level: [10], capacity_per_level: [24])),
            required_services: [],
            output_per_tick: Some(400),
            max_stock: Some(20000),
        ),
        (
            id: "small_granary",
            size: (2, 2),
            cost: 40,
            levels: 1,
            service: Some((kind: "food", range_per_level: [10], capacity_per_level: [20])),
            required_services: [],
            output_per_tick: Some(400),
            max_stock: Some(100),
        ),
        (
            id: "house",
            size: (1, 1),
            cost: 10,
            levels: 3,
            service: None,
            required_services: ["water", "food"],
        ),
        (
            id: "well",
            size: (1, 1),
            cost: 12,
            levels: 1,
            service: Some((kind: "water", range_per_level: [12], capacity_per_level: [32])),
            required_services: [],
        ),
    ],
)
